[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`if`, `switch`, `#ifdef`, `#if`, `enum`, comparison operators) returns **no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m matches** (see `ERRORS.md`). `include/lib.h` declares no flags, no mode enum, no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m context/handle struct, and no setter functions. `CMakeLists.txt` defines no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `target_compile_definitions`, so there is no compile-time configuration axis
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m either. **The option axis is empty**: there is exactly one behavior mode.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Entry points: one, and it is already the lowest level
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The public API is the single function `void premultiply(cp_image_t *img)`. There
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is no convenience wrapper vs. low-level split to worry about — this *is* the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m low-level entry point, and every row below calls it directly through the `.so`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m export.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Input-shape axes the C actually branches on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Everything the C distinguishes falls out of three expressions:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | axis | expression in the C | why it matters |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |------|---------------------|----------------|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | **trip count** | `stride = wrap32(w << 2)`; `end = wrap32(stride * h)`; iterations `= end/4` if `end > 0` else `0` | `w` and `h` only ever reach the code through this wrapping product, so the shape axis is really "which `end` does `(w,h)` produce": zero, small, exact, or wrap-truncated |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | **row-agnostic walk** | `data` is walked as a flat byte run over `[0, end)`, never per row | any `(w,h)` with the same product must give byte-identical results — a real equivalence to test |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | **value dependence** | `v/255.0f`, `* a`, `* 255.0f`, `cvttss2si` | the float round-trip and truncate-toward-zero make the output depend on the *value* of each byte, so `a = 0`, `a = 255`, and mid-range alphas are genuinely different paths |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `stride` is always a multiple of 4 and `wrap32` preserves that, so `end` is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m always a multiple of 4: **there is no partial-tail code path**. Alignment of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `cp_pixel_t` is 1 (four `uint8_t`s), so a byte-misaligned `pix` is a legal input
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m shape and gets its own rows.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Every row is exercised through both `.so` exports with **many seeded random
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m inputs** (fixed seed, reproducible), not one hand-picked value, and every
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m comparison is over the full padded buffer so out-of-span writes are caught too.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Table
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Options column is `—` on every row because the option axis is provably empty.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|----------------|-------------------------------------------|-----|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 1 | `premultiply` | — ; `w=1,h=1`, **exhaustive** sweep of all 256 channel values × all 256 alphas (65536 pixels) — full proof of the float round-trip and `cvttss2si` truncation | [x] |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 2 | `premultiply` | — ; `w=1,h=1`, exhaustive `(r,g,b)` = `(v, 255-v, v/2)` × all 256 alphas — proves the three channels are handled independently and identically | [x] |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 3 | `premultiply` | — ; `w=1,h=1`, seeded random pixels ×20000 (single-pixel minimum shape) | [x] |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 4 | `premultiply` | — ; `w=256,h=1`, ramp data `pix[i] = (i,i,i,i)` (one row, many pixels) | [x] |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 5 | `premultiply` | — ; `w=256,h=256` (65536 px), seeded random — large multi-row | [x] |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 6 | `premultiply` | — ; `w=2,h=3` seeded random ×500 (small even dims) | [x] |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 7 | `premultiply` | — ; `w=3,h=5` seeded random ×500 (odd, non-power-of-two dims) | [x] |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 8 | `premultiply` | — ; `w=7,h=1` seeded random ×500 (single row, odd width) | [x] |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 9 | `premultiply` | — ; `w=1,h=7` seeded random ×500 (single column) | [x] |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 10 | `premultiply` | — ; `w=64,h=64` seeded random ×20 | [x] |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 11 | `premultiply` | — ; `w=1000,h=1` seeded random (wide row) | [x] |
